{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. He played a significant role in the history of the Crusader states during the 12th century. \nThe *Early Life* section of his biography states that Raymond III was born around 1140 and became the Count of Tripoli after his father's death in 1152. He was still a minor at that time and the regency was exercised by King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. \nThe *War with Nur ad-Din* section describes the conflicts between Raymond III and the Muslim leader Nur ad-Din. Nur ad-Din was a powerful ruler who united the Muslim forces of Syria and threatened the Crusader states. Raymond III formed an alliance with the Byzantine Empire to counter the threat posed by Nur ad-Din. \nThe *Battle of Montgisard* section highlights the military campaigns of Raymond III. In 1177 he led a Crusader army to victory against the forces of Saladin at the Battle of Montgisard. This battle was a significant victory for the Crusaders and it halted the advance of Saladin's army into the Kingdom of Jerusalem. \nThe *Later Life and Death* section states that Raymond III was captured by Saladin's forces in 1174 and was imprisoned for several years. He was released in 1176 and resumed his position as the Count of Tripoli. Raymond III died in 1187 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV Count of Tripoli. \nThe *Legacy* section describes the impact of Raymond III's life and reign on the history of the Crusader states. He played a crucial role in the defense of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Tripoli against the Muslim forces. His military campaigns and alliances with other Christian leaders helped to maintain the balance of power in the region. \nOverall Raymond III Count of Tripoli was an important figure in the history of the Crusades and 